        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Dataset represents the HARV growth assays in the form of graphs and images. Detailed data descriptions can be found below and included with each figure legend. Treatment details follow the dataset description.
<p>
Figure 1: End-point growth curve analysis of S. mutans simulated microgravity and normal gravity HARV cultures. Simulated microgravity (dark grey bars) and normal gravity (light grey bars) HARV end-point growth was measured by serial dilution and CFU/ml plating at t = 0 (time of inoculation; average of n=4 independent experiments), and t= 4, 6, 8, 12, 24, and 48 hours’ post- inoculation (average of n=2 independent experiments per time point per growth condition). The vertical dotted line on the graph indicates that the t=24 and 48-hour data were obtained in a separate experimental run from the t=4, 6, 8, and 12-hour time points. Error bars = standard error of the mean (SEM).
<p>
Figure 2: Continuous growth curve analysis of S. mutans simulated microgravity and normal gravity HARV cultures. Each simulated microgravity (triangles) and normal gravity (circles) HARV growth curve was performed in biofilm media for 24 hours at 37°C and 5% CO2. HARVs were sampled for serial dilution and CFU/ml determination at t = 0 (time of inoculation), 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, and 24 hours’ post-inoculation, as described in Materials and Methods. Data represent n=3 independent experiments per growth condition, error bars = standard error of the mean (SEM).
<p>
Figure 3: OD600 (A), CFU/ml (B), and pH (C) data corresponding to end-point S. mutans microgravity and normal gravity HARV cultures harvested for metabolomics (t=4, 8, and 24 hours’ growth) and for RNA-seq (t=8 hours only). Data represent n=3 independent experiments per time point per growth condition. Error bars = standard error of the mean (SEM).
<p>
Figure 4: Representative S. mutans HARVs grown for 8 hours, 30 RPM, in BM under either simulated microgravity (Left) or normal gravity (Right) conditions. Note the structural differences of the self-aggregating bacterial cells under each condition (indicated by arrows).
<p>
Figure 5: Diagrams of Disposable Vessel Rotary Cell Culture Systems used to simulate microgravity (A; HARV rotated on an axis perpendicular to gravitational vector) and normal gravity (B; HARV rotated on an axis parallel to gravitational vector) growth conditions
<p>
For the continuous HARV Growth Curves experiment, overnight S. mutans cultures were used to inoculate 120 ml of sterile BM containing 11 mM glucose and 10 mM sucrose to an OD600 = 0.06. This inoculum was mixed well by vigorous swirling and vortexing, and 1 ml withdrawn to measure the time-of-inoculation (t=0) CFU/ml. The remaining inoculum volume was then subjected again to vigorous swirling and vortexing and immediately used to fill 2 x 50 ml disposable HARVs as described above. Each HARV was rotated either vertically (“simulated microgravity”) or horizontally (“normal gravity”) at 37°C, 5% CO2 for 24 hours. HARVs were rotated at 30 RPM for the first 8 hours of growth, and the rotation speed of each reactor was increased to 32 RPM after 8 hours of growth to ensure that S. mutans cell aggregates did not settle to the bottom of the simulated microgravity HARV. At 2, 4, 6, 8, 12, and 24 hours’ post-inoculation, each HARV was removed from the incubator, vigorously agitated to evenly disperse cell clumps, and 1 ml culture was immediately removed for CFU/ml assessment. The same volume of sterile media was then added back to each HARV and air bubbles removed prior to continued growth. For CFU/ml quantification, each 1 ml sample was vortexed vigorously for at least 30 seconds to resuspend cell clumps, followed immediately by serial dilution (each tube vortexed vigorously for at least 15 seconds between each dilution) and CFU/ml determination using the track plating method.
<p>
Treatment for the HARV "End-Point" Growth Curves used overnight S. mutans cultures to inoculate 500 ml sterile BM containing 11 mM glucose and 10 mM sucrose to an approximate OD600 = 0.06/ml as described above. 1 ml was withdrawn to measure the t=0 OD600, pH, and CFU/ml. The remaining inoculum volume was then immediately used to fill 8 x 50 ml disposable HARVs. For end-point growth curve analysis at t=4, 6, 8, and 12 hours conditions, 4 simulated microgravity and 4 normal gravity HARV cultures were grown in parallel at 37°C, 5% CO2. HARVs were rotated at 30 RPM for the first 8 hours of growth, and the rotation speed of each reactor was increased to 32 RPM after 8 hours of growth. At each time point, one simulated microgravity and one normal gravity HARV were each harvested for CFU/ml, OD600, and pH measurements.
        </div>

